# Design - tb-convergence-severity-calibration

**Status:** draft

GitHub issue #143 (wave 3 of tracking issue #150). Source design:
`docs/issues/tb-convergence-and-retirement/follow-up-feature.md` and that
feature's D8, D11 and D14. Settled entries are in `interview.md` (D1-D9).

## Problem

Review rounds, not round cost, are now the main convergence problem. B1 took
4 rounds and C1 took 6; most late findings needed an unusual combination of
conditions and were rated high, so they blocked.

- The reviewer instructions define low as "needs an unusual combination of
  conditions", but reviewers weigh the consequence and rate high. Nothing says
  likelihood is judged first or what "unusual" means.
- Specs state no failure model, so "outside the stated failure model" has
  nothing to point at, and unbounded criteria ("any", "always") invite an
  endless search for one more edge case.
- In light runs, strict paired review of operations prose produces narrower
  and narrower wording findings (seven rounds in one recent run).

## Intent

Reviewers rate unusual-combination and out-of-model findings low, reliably and
by a stated rule. Full-rigor specs state the conditions they handle, and
`spec.validate` refuses a spec without a failure model or with unbounded
criteria. Light-run documentation is held to a "good enough" bar: accurate
and safe to follow, not perfect prose.

## Chosen shape

**Severity calibration (D1).** The code and planning reviewer instructions
gain a likelihood-first rule. Realistic means it arises in ordinary use
within the handled conditions with no one acting unusually. A scenario that
needs two or more independent uncommon conditions at once is low whatever its
consequence; a severe consequence never raises an unusual scenario above low.
Examples of uncommon conditions are named. Instruction text only: no schema
change and no mechanical downgrade.

**Failure model in reviews (D2).** The instructions name the `Failure model`
section of `requirements/spec.md` (or `requirements/brief.md`) as the stated
failure model. A trigger outside its handled conditions is low; a failure of
the stated detect-and-refuse behaviour is in the model and is rated on its
scenario. Without the section, nothing is low merely for being outside a
model. Planning reviewers also judge whether the model has an obvious gap.
No packet change.

**Failure model in specs (AC8; D3-D5).**
- The spec template gains a required `## Failure model` section (handled
  conditions; detect-and-refuse behaviour outside them).
- `spec.validate`'s trusted parser (`planning.js`) adds two checks:
  - the section exists, is not empty and has no `TODO`;
  - the lines of the `Acceptance Criteria` and `Rubric` sections (outside
    code) don't use `always`, `any`, `all cases`, `never fails`,
    `converges` or `robust` unless the same line carries a failure-model
    reference, a numeral or a bounding phrase (`at most`, `at least`,
    `up to`, `no more than`, `within`).
  - Each failure names its line; one failure lists every offending line.
- Versioning: the policy becomes `planning-facts/2`. An old spec is
  identified by its exact bytes: a spec whose hash equals the spec of a
  validation this run recorded under an earlier policy, or one the run already
  grandfathered, gets the two checks as warnings. Every other spec is held to
  them, including a rewritten spec in an old run and the spec of a run
  resumed from an earlier activation or contract, whose old bytes cannot be
  verified. The portable `lint_spec.py` always applies them.
- `spec-draft.md`, `spec-validate.md` and PROTOCOL.md describe the section and
  the lint.

**Light runs (D6, D7).** The brief may carry an optional `## Failure model`
section, with no lint. The brief-based reviewer instructions add the
documentation bar: a finding about documentation or operations prose blocks
only when a reader following it as written, in a realistic scenario, reaches
a wrong or unsafe outcome (wrong command, flag, path or value; a missing
warning before a destructive or irreversible step; a contradiction with the
shipped code or the brief). Wording, style, structure, narrower restatements,
rare unmentioned edge cases and suggestions to document more are low. The bar
never lowers the brief: documentation the brief's goal or scope asks for is a
requirement, and a missing or wrong part of it is judged like any other unmet
requirement.

**Contract version (D8).** `REVIEW_CONTRACT_VERSION` moves, so retained
results of in-flight runs keep their recorded qualification on resume.

## Alternatives considered

- A structured `conditions` field with a mechanical downgrade (D1): changes
  the verdict schema #142 is editing, and the correction policy forbids
  meaning-changing corrections.
- A `packet.failureModel` field (D2): edits packet assembly owned by #142;
  the reviewer already has the document.
- Grandfathering a whole run, an opt-in spec marker, or a date cutoff, for
  versioning (D5): a whole run lets a rewritten spec skip the rule, a marker
  lets new specs skip it, and dates are not protocol facts.
- Mechanical demotion of `*.md` findings, single-reviewer docs review, or the
  docs bar in full runs too (D7): meaning-changing, a protocol gate change, and
  beyond the issue respectively.

## Constraints

- GateReeve corrects a verdict only where the answer follows from the verdict
  itself. Low findings already never block; this work changes what reviewers
  are told, not how GateReeve rewrites their verdicts.
- Edits to files #142 owns (`execution.js`) stay limited to the instruction
  text and the contract version constant. No edit to `boundary-review.js`,
  `local-agent-adapters.js`, packet assembly, sweep records or round-policy
  dispositions.
- Existing runs keep working: a run whose spec was validated under the earlier
  policy gets warnings for that spec, and retained reviews keep their
  qualification. This implements D11's "a resumed run on an old spec gets a
  warning" for every old spec on record (the upgrade of an in-flight run). A
  run resumed from an earlier activation or contract has no record of its old
  spec, so it adds the section before validating; this narrowing of D11 is an
  agent decision (interview D5) called out on the PR.
- The desktop's staged copy of the protocol stays byte-identical to the
  plugin source.

## Open risks

- Instruction changes shift reviewer behaviour statistically, not certainly.
  A reviewer may still rate an unusual scenario high; the higher rating still
  wins between peers. The metrics store will show whether late-round high
  findings fall.
- A real defect could be rated low because the reviewer judged a condition
  "uncommon" that is common in practice. The failure scenario states the
  conditions, so a human can see and overturn it.
- The word lint has false positives and can be satisfied superficially
  (accepted in D11).
- A run resumed from an earlier activation or contract stops at
  `spec.validate` until its spec gains a failure model and bounded criteria.
  The diagnostic names each line, so the fix is quick, but it is a stop D11
  did not foresee.
- The documentation bar could let a misleading runbook through if a reviewer
  misjudges what "following it as written" leads to.

